🟢 Slide 1: Opening – Why This Matters (45 sec)

“Today I want to walk you through how New Relic alerting works end-to-end, and more importantly, how we can use it to reduce noise, improve signal quality, and ultimately reduce MTTR across our systems.

At a high level, New Relic alerting is not just about triggering alerts — it’s about transforming raw telemetry into actionable insights, routing them intelligently, and ensuring the right teams get the right signal at the right time.

What I’ll cover today is the full alert lifecycle — from signal detection all the way to notification — and where we can optimize it.”

⸻

🟢 Slide 2: 10,000-Foot View (1 min)

“Let’s start with the big picture.

Everything begins with signal data — metrics, logs, traces, synthetics, or business events.

That data flows into the alert engine, where we define conditions to detect anomalies or threshold breaches.

Once a condition is met:
	•	An incident is created
	•	Incidents are grouped into issues to reduce noise
	•	Issues are passed into workflows
	•	And finally routed to destinations like Slack, PagerDuty, or ServiceNow

So the pipeline is:
Signal → Condition → Incident → Issue → Workflow → Notification

The key value here is not just detection — it’s noise reduction and intelligent routing.”

⸻

🟢 Slide 3: What is a Signal? (1 min)

“A signal is essentially any NRQL query result over time.

This is very powerful because:
	•	If we can query it, we can alert on it
	•	It supports faceting, which allows us to monitor multiple entities independently

For example:
	•	CPU per host
	•	Error rate per service
	•	Latency per API endpoint

Each facet becomes its own signal stream.

This is where scale comes in — but also where noise can explode if not handled properly.”

⸻

🟢 Slide 4: Conditions – Detection Layer (1.5 min)

“Conditions are where we define what ‘bad’ looks like.

Each condition has:
	•	A signal query (NRQL)
	•	A threshold (warning or critical)
	•	A time window

For example:
	•	Error rate > 5% for 5 minutes
	•	Latency > 2 seconds sustained

Now, an important nuance:
New Relic does not trigger instantly — it evaluates over a window, which helps reduce false positives.

This is where a lot of tuning happens:
	•	Too sensitive → noise
	•	Too relaxed → missed issues

So conditions are really the first control point for signal quality.”

⸻

🟢 Slide 5: Incidents (Events) (1 min)

“When a signal breaches a threshold, an incident is opened.

A few key behaviors:
	•	Incidents open automatically when threshold is breached
	•	They close automatically when signal recovers
	•	One incident is created per facet

So if we are faceting by host, and 10 hosts fail — we get 10 incidents.

This is powerful for granularity — but also where alert volume can spike quickly.”

⸻

🟢 Slide 6: Issues – Noise Reduction Layer (1.5 min)

“This is where New Relic becomes very powerful.

Instead of sending every incident as a notification, it groups incidents into issues.

Think of incidents as symptoms — and issues as the actual problem.

For example:
	•	10 pods failing → 10 incidents
	•	But grouped into 1 issue → ‘Service degradation’

This reduces noise significantly.

Issues:
	•	Open when incidents open
	•	Can contain multiple incidents
	•	Close automatically when all incidents resolve

This layer is critical for:
	•	Reducing alert fatigue
	•	Improving triage efficiency
	•	Giving a higher-level view of problems”

⸻

🟢 Slide 7: Policies – Grouping Strategy (1 min)

“Policies control how incidents are grouped into issues.

There are three main strategies:
	1.	Per Policy → everything grouped together
	2.	Per Condition → grouped by condition
	3.	Per Incident → no grouping (maximum granularity)

Choosing the right strategy is key:
	•	Too much grouping → loss of detail
	•	Too little grouping → alert explosion

So policies define our noise vs visibility tradeoff.”

⸻

🟢 Slide 8: Workflows – Routing Intelligence (1.5 min)

“Once an issue is created, it flows into workflows.

Workflows determine:
	•	Who gets notified
	•	When they get notified
	•	What information they receive

This is where we can implement intelligent routing, for example:
	•	Route infra alerts to platform team
	•	Route application alerts to service owners
	•	Route critical alerts differently from warnings

Workflows also support:
	•	Payload customization
	•	Filtering logic
	•	Enrichment with additional metadata

This is where alerting becomes context-aware, not just reactive.”

⸻

🟢 Slide 9: Destinations (45 sec)

“Destinations are simply where notifications go:
	•	Slack
	•	PagerDuty
	•	ServiceNow
	•	Email

They are reusable and decoupled from workflows.

This allows us to:
	•	Standardize integrations
	•	Reuse configurations
	•	Scale notification strategy easily”

⸻

🟢 Slide 10: Muting Rules – Critical for Noise Control (1.5 min)

“This is one of the most important features for real-world operations — muting rules.

Muting rules allow us to suppress alerts:
	•	During maintenance windows
	•	During deployments
	•	For known issues

They can be scoped by:
	•	Entity
	•	Tags
	•	Conditions

And can be time-based or dynamic.

Without muting rules:
	•	Teams get flooded with expected alerts
	•	Trust in alerting drops

With muting:
	•	We maintain signal quality
	•	Reduce fatigue
	•	Ensure alerts are meaningful”

⸻

🟢 Slide 11: Workflow Automation (1.5 min)

“Beyond basic routing, workflows enable automation.

We can:
	•	Enrich alerts with metadata
	•	Customize payloads for different systems
	•	Route dynamically based on attributes

For example:
	•	Add service owner info
	•	Include runbook links
	•	Trigger downstream automation

This is where alerting integrates into a broader incident response ecosystem.

It’s not just notifying — it’s enabling faster resolution.”

⸻

🟢 Slide 12: Signals to Alert On (1 min)

“In terms of what we should alert on, we focus on high-value signals:
	•	Infrastructure metrics (CPU, memory)
	•	APM golden signals (latency, errors, throughput)
	•	Key transactions
	•	Synthetic monitoring
	•	Business metrics

The key principle:
👉 Not everything should alert
👉 Only what impacts reliability or customer experience”

⸻

🟢 Slide 13: Closing – Business Impact (1 min)

“To summarize:

New Relic alerting provides:
	•	End-to-end visibility from signal to notification
	•	Built-in noise reduction via issues
	•	Intelligent routing via workflows
	•	Strong control via muting rules

When implemented correctly, this leads to:
	•	Reduced alert fatigue
	•	Faster triage
	•	Lower MTTR
	•	Better operational efficiency

The opportunity for us is to standardize and optimize these layers so that alerting becomes a trusted and scalable system, not a source of noise.”

